503:
(a) Any person who is withdrawing, obtaining, or utilizing groundwater and who is required under section 26-62 to obtain a permit shall submit an application for a permit to use groundwater to the city council, on forms prepared and furnished upon request by the city council. The applicant shall furnish the city council with sufficient documented evidence as set forth in this section to evaluate the effects of the described water use upon the water resources within the corporate limits of the city.
(b) Any person intending to withdraw, obtain, or utilize groundwater and who is required under section 26-62 to obtain a permit shall submit an application for a permit to use groundwater to the city council, on forms prepared and furnished, upon request, by the city council through the city clerk, or any other properly designated agent. No groundwater shall be withdrawn, obtained, or utilized until a permit has been granted (except as provided in section 26-62). The applicant shall furnish the city council with sufficient documented evidence as set forth in this section to evaluate the impacts of the proposed water use upon the water resources within the corporate limits of the city.
(c) During the early stages of planning for a proposed groundwater withdrawal and in any case prior to the start of well construction, the intended user should have a conference with the mayor or any other duly designated agent of the city, including, but not limited to, any member of the city council, to determine the acceptability of the proposed well, the aquifer to be utilized, the well spacing and well depth, and the amount of intended groundwater use. Such conference shall be granted within 30 days after request therefor. The intended user should furnish the city council, through the mayor or any other duly designated agent with such available, documented evidence as set forth in subsections (d) through (f) of this section for presentation at the conference or for later submittal, but prior to well construction. Based, in part, on the information furnished by the intended user and upon other considerations as outlined within this article, the city council may deny the permit or may approve the permit upon such terms and conditions as the city council may deem necessary to protect the public water system of the city and to ensure an adequate public water supply. If necessary to provide to the city council sufficient information in order to make a decision as to the issuance of a permit for groundwater use, an applicant may construct a well at the applicant's own risk and expense. Upon completion of the construction of the well and prior to any groundwater use, the intended user shall submit an application for a groundwater use permit to the city council pursuant to sections 26-64 and 26-65. The construction of any well by the applicant will be at the applicant's own risk prior to the grant of a groundwater use permit pursuant to sections 26-64 and 26-65, and the construction of said well shall not grant to applicant any vested right to withdraw, obtain or utilize groundwater until the issuance of a permit in accordance with section 26-65.
(d) Sufficient documented evidence shall include, but not be limited to, the following (except for farm use applications):
(1) Name and address of the applicant;
(2) The location of the existing or proposed well, identified by number, for which the permit is requested, marked on the best map available;
(3) The latitude and longitude, to the nearest ten seconds, of the well obtained from the location map;
(4) The closest public street adjacent to the property upon which the well is located;
(5) The ground elevation of the well, if available;
(6) The amount of water withdrawn or proposed to be withdrawn, or used, and a statement to indicate the extent to which such use or withdrawal is reasonably necessary to meet the needs of the applicant;
(7) Any present or anticipated unreasonable adverse effects or potential unreasonable adverse effects on other water uses or users, including, but not limited to, adverse effects on public use, including, but not limited to, the public water system of the city, or farm use, or both;
(8) A statement specifying the beneficial use of the groundwater withdrawn or to be withdrawn and whether the water use is a consumptive or nonconsumptive use, as herein defined;
(9) If the water use is, or proposes to be, a nonconsumptive use, the applicant shall state the following:
a. The treatment of the water and procedure used, or the proposed treatment of the water and the procedure to be used, to return the water to the aquifer or groundwater system from which it is, or proposed to be, withdrawn;
b. The location of the injection well to be shown on the same map as the existing, or proposed, withdrawal wells;
c. The chemical, physical, and bacteriological quality, and any other specified water quality analysis, the division or the city, or both, may require, of the return water, noting specifically any substantial impairment of the water quality from the water withdrawn;
d. The aquifer or groundwater system from which the groundwater is withdrawn, or intended to be withdrawn, and the amount of water to be returned to the aquifer or groundwater system; and
e. Any substantial decrease in quantity as originally withdrawn from the aquifer or groundwater system.
(10) The acquifer from which the groundwater is withdrawn, or intended to be withdrawn;
(11) For existing wells, besides the above information, the well construction data for each well on forms provided by the city, including, but not limited to, the following information:
a. Name of driller;
b. Date of drilling;
c. Total well depth in feet;
d. Diameter of drilled hole;
e. Diameter and depth of casing, including casing material;
f. Depth of grouting;
g. Diameter and depth of the setting of the well screen, if used, and the material and type of screen;
h. Type of permanent pump, size, horsepower, and yield;
i. Static water level and pumping level; and
j. Number of hours the well is pumped per day.
(12) A copy of any and all applications and information supplied by the applicant to the division, the state department of agriculture, and any other governmental entity (state or federal), and said entity's responses and determinations, as to any groundwater use within the corporate limits of the city.
(e) For farm use, sufficient documented evidence shall include, but not be limited to, the following:
(1) Applicant's full name;
(2) Mailing address;
(3) Closest public street adjacent to the premises upon which the existing or proposed well is located;
(4) Purpose of withdrawal;
(5) Maximum day withdrawal;
(6) Well construction data, including, but not limited to, the following:
a. Well depth;
b. Depth of pump intake below ground surface;
c. Design pumping capacity of well; and
d. Depth of well casing.
(7) Month and year of well pump installation;
(8) Number of acres irrigated from this well and average number of inches of water applied from this well per year;
(9) Whether or not chemicals, fertilizers, fungicides, herbicides, insecticides, or nematicides are injected into the irrigation water;
(10) Map showing the location of the water source; and
(11) A copy of any and all applications and information supplied by the applicant to the division, the state department of agriculture, and any other governmental entity (state or federal), and said entity's responses and determinations, as to any groundwater use within the corporate limits of the city.
(f) Any person submitting an application for a groundwater use permit who is at the time of application withdrawing, obtaining, or utilizing groundwater and who is unable to furnish accurate information concerning the amounts of groundwater being withdrawn or used shall be required, as the city council deems necessary, to do the following:
(1) Install one or more water meters; or
(2) Some other more economical means acceptable to the city council for measuring the groundwater withdrawn or used.
(g) The city council, in determining the amount of groundwater withdrawn or used by an applicant, may use one or more of the following:
(1) The rated capacity of the pump;
(2) The rated capacity of the cooling system;
(3) The standards or methods employed by the United States Geological Survey in determining such quantities; or
(4) Any other acceptable method.
(h) Except for farm use, those applicants intending to withdraw, obtain, or utilize groundwater from wells constructed after December 31, 1994, shall submit, in addition to the information required in subsection (d) of this section, well construction details and specifications, as the city council deems necessary, including, but not limited to, the following:
(1) Total depth of well, in feet;
(2) Size of drilled hole;
(3) Size and depth of casing and type of casing material;
(4) Size and depth of the placing of the well screen and the type of material, if used;
(5) Depth of grouting;
(6) Deep well airline of steel, iron, or heavy-gauge copper material, with screw cap, for water level measurement; and
(7) Filling, plugging, and sealing procedures for any well that is to be abandoned.
(i) In the event that any groundwater within the corporate limits of the city has a corrosive nature, proposed well construction specifications and casing material may require modification as the city council deems necessary to avoid any potential unreasonable adverse effects on the aquifer or groundwater system, or of other water uses, including, but not limited to, the public water system within the city.
(Ord. No. 108, § 4, 11-21-1994)

504:
(a) In considering permit applications, revocations, and modifications, the city council shall consider:
(1) The number of persons using an aquifer and the object, extent, and necessity of their respective withdrawals or uses;
(2) The nature and size of the aquifer;
(3) The physical and chemical nature of any impairment of the aquifer, adversely affecting its availability or fitness for other water uses (including public and farm use);
(4) The probable severity and duration of such impairment under foreseeable conditions;
(5) The physical and chemical nature of any impairment of the aquifer utilized to provide groundwater for the public water system of the city adversely affecting its availability or fitness for said use;
(6) The probable severity and duration of such impairment under foreseeable conditions;
(7) The injury to public health, safety, or welfare which results if such impairment were not prevented or evaded;
(8) The kinds of businesses or activities to which the various uses are related;
(9) The importance and necessity of the uses claimed by the permit applicants, or of the water uses within the corporate limits of the city and the extent of any injury or detriment caused or expected to be caused to other water uses (including public and farm use);
(10) The importance and necessity of the water use of the groundwater to provide a public water system by the city, and the extent of any injury or detriment caused or expected to be caused to said system upon a grant of the permit to a private or public (other than the city) applicant;
(11) Division from or reduction of flows in other watercourses or aquifers;
(12) Any other relevant factors, such as, but not limited to, the best geologic and hydrologic information available on the aquifer or groundwater system within the corporate limits of the city.
(b) A permit applicant bears the burden of proof as to each of the standards set out in subsection (a) of this section and that the granting of a permit to the applicant will not have an unreasonably adverse effect upon other water uses within the corporate limits of the city, including, but not limited to, the public water system of the city, including unreasonably adverse effects upon the potential as well as present use of the groundwater within the corporate limits of the city. Failure by the applicant to meet the burden of proof or submit sufficient information shall be grounds for the city council to deny a permit application, or issue a temporary permit in accordance with section 26-65(c).
(c) In any case where a permit applicant can prove to the city council's satisfaction that the applicant was withdrawing or using groundwater prior to December 31, 1994, the city council shall take into consideration the extent to which the prior use or withdrawal was reasonably necessary in the judgment of the city council to meet the applicant's needs and the applicant's investment in land, construction of the well and anticipated usage of the groundwater, and shall grant a permit which shall meet the reasonable needs, unless the granting of such permit will have unreasonably adverse effects upon other water uses within the corporate limits of the city, including, but not limited to, the public water system of the city, including unreasonably adverse effects upon the potential as well as present use of the groundwater within the corporate limits of the city. In determining whether a permit will have an unreasonable adverse effect, the city council shall consider the standards set forth in subsection (a) of this section.
(d) The city council shall have the power to:
(1) Modify or revoke any permit when the city council deems necessary upon not less than 60 days' written notice to any person affected when the groundwater use or withdrawal is not in compliance with the terms of the permit or when there is found to be an unreasonable adverse affect upon the water uses or users in the area, including the public water system of the city, and including potential as well as present use, based upon the considerations set forth in subsection (a) of this section; or
(2) Deny a permit application if the application therefor or the effect of the water use proposed or described therein upon the water resources of the area is found to be contrary to the public interest or general welfare. Such a decision shall be based upon the considerations and standards set forth in subsection (a) of this section.
(Ord. No. 108, § 5, 11-21-1994)

505:
(a) When sufficient evidence is provided by the applicant that the water withdrawn or used, or intended to be withdrawn or used, is a nonconsumptive use, as defined in section 26-61, a permit therefor shall be issued by the city council without the conditions set forth in subsection (d) of this section; provided, however, that in determining whether a use of groundwater is nonconsumptive, the city council may take into consideration, based on the best geologic and hydrologic information available, whether any material injury or detriment to other water users of the area by reason of the reduction of water pressure in the aquifer or system has not been adequately compensated by the permit applicant who caused or substantially contributed to such injury or detriment. Any permit issued for a nonconsumptive use does not imply consent to inject any waste or pollutant material into the groundwater system.
(b) When the water withdrawn or used, or proposed to be withdrawn or used, by the permit applicant is a consumptive use and when there are not unreasonable adverse effects upon other water uses, including the public water system of the city and including potential as well as present use, based upon the standards set forth in section 26-64(a), a permit to use groundwater shall be issued by the city council containing such conditions as set forth in subsection (d) of this section as the city council deems necessary to the development and use of the water resources. The permit shall only be valid for one year from the date of approval by the city council.
(c) The city council may grant any temporary permit for less than a year as the city council shall specify where conditions make such temporary permit essential. A temporary permit may be issued whenever the city council has insufficient information to evaluate the effects of a permit application as set forth in section 26-64(a).
(d) A permit for a consumptive use may contain, but not be limited to, one or more of the following conditions:
(1) Total permitted well depth in feet;
(2) The aquifer or groundwater system to be utilized;
(3) Maximum pumping rate;
(4) Maximum day withdrawal;
(5) Pumping level (elevation below which water may not be pumped);
(6) Amount of groundwater to be withdrawn or used;
(7) Well spacing to minimize well interference;
(8) Time of withdrawal;
(9) Require observation or monitoring well to be installed for monitoring groundwater levels and water quality.
(e) Water withdrawn under any permit shall be used only for the purposes set forth in the permit.
(f) The permit holder shall notify the city council by registered letter of any changes in the beneficial use, or if a greater amount of water is desired to be withdrawn. A permit may be modified by the city council at the request of the permit holder after the city council has considered the standards set forth in section 26-64(a).
(Ord. No. 108, § 6, 11-21-1994)

507:
(a) Every person who has obtained a permit to use groundwater shall file with the city council with any renewal application, a certified statement of the quantities of water used or withdrawn, sources of water, and the nature of the use thereof, which occurred during the term of the permit. The certified statement (report) shall include, but not be limited to, the following:
(1) Name of permit holder and permit number;
(2) Beneficial use of groundwater use;
(3) Source of groundwater, identifying the aquifer utilized and identifying the well using each aquifer;
(4) Quantity of water used or withdrawn monthly from each aquifer (if a well penetrates more than one aquifer, it is to be noted);
(5) Average hours pumped per day;
(6) The static and pumping levels of each aquifer utilized and the date the water levels were measured; the measurements are to be made during the last month of the term of the current permit; and
(7) For a nonconsumptive use, the amount of water returned to the aquifer or groundwater system from which the water is withdrawn.
(b) The permit holder shall have a specific conductance analysis of the groundwater from the well performed by a laboratory acceptable to the city council, when the permit holder seeks a renewal application, and a copy of the report shall be submitted to the city council with the renewal application and the groundwater use report. The city council may also collect water samples from the well and perform specific conductance analysis as it deems necessary. The specific conductance analysis shall be according to a method acceptable to the city council. The city council, considering the information contained within the original application, the renewal application, the groundwater use report, and the standards of section 26-64(a), may waive the requirement of a specific conductance analysis for a renewal application.
(c) If any person who is required to submit a groundwater use report is unable to furnish accurate information concerning the amounts of groundwater being withdrawn or used, or if there is evidence that a report is false or inaccurate, or if a person is withdrawing or using a larger quantity or under different conditions than permitted, the city council shall take such action as it deems necessary, including, but not limited to, any one of the following:
(1) In determining the amount of groundwater being withdrawn or used;
a. Require the person to install one or more water meters;
b. Require the person to use some other economical means acceptable to the city council for measuring the groundwater use;
c. Require the person to use the rated capacity of the pump;
d. Require the person to use the rated capacity of the cooling system;
e. Require the person to use the standards or methods employed by the United States Geological Survey in determining such quantities;
f. Any other acceptable method.
(2) Require the person to withdraw or use only the amount of groundwater permitted; or
(3) Revoke or amend the permit.
(Ord. No. 108, § 8, 11-21-1994)

509:
To protect against or abate brackish water (due to salt or otherwise) encroachment or deterioration of the water quality of the groundwater, the city council shall take into consideration the best information on the geologic and hydrologic characteristics of the rocks and the groundwater withdrawals of the area involved and shall require the user to take such action as it deems necessary for control of brackish water (due to salt or otherwise) intrusion into freshwater aquifers. The control measures may include, but not be limited to, the following:
(1) Pumping arrangements to reduce groundwater withdrawal in areas of concentrated pumping;
(2) Rearrangement of the location of wells to eliminate or reduce concentrated areas of groundwater withdrawal;
(3) Requirement of selective withdrawal from other available freshwater aquifers than presently used;
(4) Curtailment of groundwater withdrawal of proposed water users in the area;
(5) Selective curtailment or reduction of groundwater withdrawal where it is found to be in the public interest or general welfare to protect the water resources;
(6) Conjunctive use of freshwater or brackish water aquifers, or waters of less desirable quality, where water quality of a specific character is not essential;
(7) Use of observation or monitoring wells, drilled into freshwater aquifers between areas of groundwater withdrawal and the location of brackish water zones, to detect the movement of brackish water towards the freshwater aquifers, or to detect the deterioration of water quality;
(8) Use of interceptor wells, drilled into the area of encroachment, to intercept the brackish water toward the center of excessive groundwater withdrawal;
(9) Use of relief wells, drilled into the brackish water aquifer, to relieve hydraulic pressure in the aquifer causing encroachment;
(10) Plugging with cement of deep wells that have penetrated brackish water zones or zones of undesirable water quality, where hydraulic pressures cause leakage and contaminate freshwater aquifers of lower pressures;
(11) Abandonment of wells which are then to be filled, plugged, and sealed; and
(12) Such other control or abatement techniques as are technically feasible and proven to be successful in other areas and nationally.
(Ord. No. 108, § 10, 11-21-1994)

510:
In order to protect against or abate unreasonable adverse or potential adverse effects on other water users within the corporate limits of the city, including, but not limited to, the public water system of the city, the city council shall take into consideration the best information available on the geologic and hydrologic characteristics of the rocks and groundwater withdrawals of the area and shall require the user to take such actions as it deems necessary. The control measures which the city council may require the user to take may include, but not be limited to, the following:
(1) Selective withdrawal from other freshwater aquifers then presently utilized;
(2) Pumping arrangements to reduce groundwater withdrawal in areas of concentrated pumping;
(3) Selective curtailment or reduction of groundwater withdrawal where it is found to be in the public interest to protect the water resources;
(4) Selective curtailment or reduction of groundwater withdrawal or revocation of the permit where it is found to be in the public interest to protect the public water system of the city; and
(5) Such other control techniques as are technically feasible and proven successful in other areas and nationally.
(Ord. No. 108, § 11, 11-21-1994)

519:
The purpose of this article is to protect the public health, safety, environment and general welfare through the regulation of nonstormwater discharges to the city separate storm sewer system to the maximum extent practicable as required by federal law. This article establishes methods for controlling the introduction of pollutants into the city separate storm sewer system in order to comply with the requirements of the National Pollutant Discharge Elimination System (NPDES) permit process. The objectives of this article are:
(1) To regulate the contribution of pollutants to the city separate storm sewer system by any person;
(2) To prohibit illicit discharges and illegal connections to the city separate storm sewer system;
(3) To prevent nonstormwater discharges, generated as a result of spills, inappropriate dumping or disposal, to the city separate storm sewer system; and
(4) To establish legal authority to carry out all inspection, surveillance, monitoring and enforcement procedures necessary to ensure compliance with this article.
(Ord. No. 05-323, § 2.1, 10-27-2005)

523:
The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this article, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Accidental discharge means a discharge prohibited by this article which occurs by chance and without planning or thought prior to occurrence.
City separate storm sewer system means any facility designed or used for collecting and/or conveying stormwater, including, but not limited to, any roads with drainage systems, highways, city streets, curbs, gutters, inlets, catchbasins, piped storm drains, pumping facilities, structural stormwater controls, ditches, swales, natural and manmade or altered drainage channels, reservoirs, and other drainage structures, and which are:
(1) Owned or maintained by the city; or
(2) Not a combined sewer; and
(3) Not part of a publicly-owned treatment works.
Clean Water Act means the Federal Water Pollution Control Act (33 USC 1251 et seq.) and any subsequent amendments thereto.
Construction activity means activities subject to the Georgia Erosion and Sedimentation Control Act or NPDES general construction permits. These include construction projects resulting in land disturbance. Such activities include but are not limited to clearing and grubbing, grading, excavating, and demolition.
Illegal connection means either of the following:
(1) Any pipe, open channel, drain or conveyance, whether on the surface or subsurface, which allows an illicit discharge to enter the storm drain system including, but not limited to, any conveyances which allow any nonstormwater discharge including sewage, process wastewater, and wash water to enter the storm drain system, regardless of whether such pipe, open channel, drain or conveyance has been previously allowed, permitted, or approved by an authorized enforcement agency; or
(2) Any pipe, open channel, drain or conveyance connected to the city separate storm sewer system which has not been documented in plans, maps, or equivalent records and approved by an authorized enforcement agency.
Illicit discharge means any direct or indirect nonstormwater discharge to the city storm sewer system, except as exempted in section 26-123(b).
Industrial activity means activities subject to NPDES industrial permits as defined in 40 CFR 122.26(b)(14).
National Pollutant Discharge Elimination System (NPDES) stormwater discharge permit means a permit issued by the Georgia EPD under authority delegated pursuant to 33 USC 1342(b) that authorizes the discharge of pollutants to waters of the United States, whether the permit is applicable on an individual, group, or general area-wide basis.
Nonstormwater discharge means any discharge to the storm drain system that is not composed entirely of stormwater.
Person means, except to the extent exempted from this article, any individual, partnership, firm, association, joint venture, public or private corporation, trust, estate, commission, board, public or private institution, utility, cooperative, city, county or other political subdivision of the state, any interstate body or any other legal entity.
Pollutant means anything which causes or contributes to pollution. Pollutants may include, but are not limited to:
(1) Paints, varnishes, and solvents;
(2) Petroleum hydrocarbons;
(3) Automotive fluids;
(4) Cooking grease;
(5) Detergents (biodegradable or otherwise);
(6) Degreasers;
(7) Cleaning chemicals;
(8) Nonhazardous liquid and solid wastes and yard wastes;
(9) Refuse, rubbish, garbage, litter, or other discarded or abandoned objects and accumulations, so that same may cause or contribute to pollution;
(10) Floatables;
(11) Pesticides, herbicides, and fertilizers;
(12) Liquid and solid wastes;
(13) Sewage, fecal coliform and pathogens;
(14) Dissolved and particulate metals;
(15) Animal wastes;
(16) Wastes and residues that result from constructing a building or structure;
(17) Concrete and cement; and
(18) Noxious or offensive matter of any kind.
Pollution means the contamination or other alteration of any water's physical, chemical or biological properties by the addition of any constituent and includes, but is not limited to, a change in temperature, taste, color, turbidity, or odor of such waters, or the discharge of any liquid, gaseous, solid, radioactive, or other substance into any such waters as will or is likely to create a nuisance or render such waters harmful, detrimental or injurious to the public health, safety, welfare, or environment, or to domestic, commercial, industrial, agricultural, recreational, or other legitimate beneficial uses, or to livestock, wild animals, birds, fish or other aquatic life.
Premises means any building, lot, parcel of land, or portion of land whether improved or unimproved including adjacent sidewalks and parking strips.
State waters means any and all rivers, streams, creeks, branches, lakes, reservoirs, ponds, drainage systems, springs, wells, and other bodies of surface and subsurface water, natural or artificial, lying within or forming a part of the boundaries of the state which are not entirely confined and retained completely upon the property of a single person.
Stormwater runoff or stormwater means any surface flow, runoff, and drainage consisting entirely of water from any form of natural precipitation, and resulting from such precipitation.
Structural stormwater control means a structural stormwater management facility or device that controls stormwater runoff and changes the characteristics of that runoff including, but not limited to, the quantity and quality, the period of release or the velocity of flow.
(Ord. No. 05-323, § 3, 10-27-2005)

526:
(a) No person shall throw, drain, or otherwise discharge, cause, or allow others under his control to throw, drain, or otherwise discharge into the city separate storm sewer system any pollutants or waters containing any pollutants, other than stormwater.
(b) The following discharges are exempt from the prohibition provision in subsection (a) of this section:
(1) Water line flushing performed by a government agency, other potable water sources, landscape irrigation or lawn watering, diverted stream flows, rising groundwater, groundwater infiltration to storm drains, uncontaminated pumped groundwater, foundation or footing drains (not including active groundwater dewatering systems), crawl space pumps, air conditioning condensation, springs, natural riparian habitat or wetland flows, and any other water source not containing pollutants;
(2) Discharges or flows from firefighting, and other discharges specified in writing by the county fire department or city as being necessary to protect public health and safety;
(3) The prohibition provision in subsection (a) of this section shall not apply to any nonstormwater discharge permitted under an NPDES permit or order issued to the discharger and administered under the authority of the state and the federal Environmental Protection Agency, provided that the discharger is in full compliance with all requirements of the permit, waiver, or order and other applicable laws and regulations, and provided that written approval has been granted for any discharge to the city separate storm sewer system.
(Ord. No. 05-323, § 4.1, 10-27-2005)

536:
Whenever the city finds that a violation of this article has occurred, the city may order compliance by written notice of violation.
(1) The notice of violation shall contain:
a. The name and address of the alleged violator;
b. The address when available or a description of the building, structure or land upon which the violation is occurring, or has occurred;
c. A statement specifying the nature of the violation;
d. A description of the remedial measures necessary to restore compliance with this article and a time schedule for the completion of such remedial action;
e. A statement of the penalty that shall or may be assessed against the person to whom the notice of violation is directed; and
f. A statement that the determination of violation may be appealed to the city council by filing a written notice of appeal within 30 days of service of notice of violation (except that in the event the violation constitutes an immediate danger to public health or public safety, 24 hours' notice shall be sufficient).
(2) Such notice may require without limitation:
a. The performance of monitoring, analyses, and reporting;
b. The elimination of illicit discharges and illegal connections;
c. That violating discharges, practices, or operations shall cease and desist;
d. The abatement or remediation of stormwater pollution or contamination hazards and the restoration of any affected property;
e. Payment of costs to cover administrative and abatement costs; and
f. The implementation of pollution prevention practices.
(Ord. No. 05-323, § 8.2, 10-27-2005)

549:
The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this article, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Litter means any organic or inorganic waste material, rubbish, refuse, cigarette butts, garbage, trash, hulls, peelings, debris, grass, weeds, ashes, sand, gravel, slag, brickbats, metal, plastic, and glass containers, broken glass, dead animals or intentionally or unintentionally discarded materials of every kind and description which are not waste as such term is defined in O.C.G.A. § 16-7-51(6).
Public or private property means:
(1) The right-of-way of any road or highway;
(2) Any body of water or watercourse or the shores or beaches thereof;
(3) Any park, playground, building, refuge, or conservation or recreation area;
(4) Timberlands or forests; and
(5) Residential, commercial, industrial, or farm properties.
(Ord. No. 05-324, § 3, 10-27-2005)
